Z||000003||David Bartlett||11-17-2001||03:38 PM||||Mach10, your jamming the signal? Give me specs!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Sootah||11-17-2001||05:56 PM||||I'm not sure if their new-fangled Ka band digital radar can be jammed. Correctly, anyway.<P>I still want specs tho. Also, what do you do about Lidar?<P>FieroHoho?<BR>7?<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sootah__Nuclear_Happy_Face_Sig.jpg">       <BR><sup><A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sootah__Black_Beauty_-_Front_Right.jpg" TARGET=_blank>85 GT</A> - <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sootah__Busted-Ass_SE.jpg" TARGET=_blank>86 SE</A> (Parts car)<BR><I>Never pull your pants down in Cosco, the dang monkeys will getcha</I></sup> <BR><sup><B>AIM</B>: KSSouter</sup>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Mach10||11-17-2001||06:40 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by David Bartlett:<BR><B>Mach10, your jamming the signal? Give me specs!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Nah... if I WAS, that's what I would do... It's actually fairly easy... but also EXCEEDINGLY illegal... That kind of power will disrupt communications all over the place. Not to mention being easy to track you down. I don't recommend it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000006||Mach10||11-17-2001||06:53 PM||||Oh, and don't forget that most newer Radar guns will sound an alarm if they detect jamming...<P>What I'd REALLY like is a directional EMP. Let 'em clock me, FRY their radar, and ask them to show me that it works. ||||reg||10
Z||000007||David Bartlett||11-17-2001||07:08 PM||||I still can't believe that nobody but me has a custom install.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Brandon86SE||11-17-2001||07:14 PM||||I don't have one yet, but have been interested ever since I got pulled over by that ghost cop car.  I'd be interested in seeing how you did yours, it'd be nice for it to be clean like that.  As far as you not getting enough power 'cause of extending the wires, just use a heavier gauge wire for both your power and ground lines.  Less resistance in bigger wires, so you'll get more true voltage to the unit.<P>Brandon||||reg||1
Z||000009||grinthock||11-18-2001||12:58 AM||||I thought about hiding mine.<P>I love those valentine one units, a tad expensive, I picked up a Whistler 1660 in PA for like $60 on my way back to Canada.  <P>It has VG-2 Steath and has helped me out (Honestly I would rather have a valentine or another one that is VG-2 IMMUNE again I do'nt have the cash)<P>here is what I do.<P>By the time your detector detects LASER in the city you are cought, same with the highway.<P>In the city, I do'nt leave it on my dash, I put it on the gauge pod just to the right of the steering wheel.  A common place.<P>I do'nt put it there in the city, most cops catch you by SEEING the unit or seeing you take it off to hide, I just leave it on the centre console, gives AMPLE warning to radar even there.<P>Here in Canada the highway speed traps ALL USE laser, but then again I watchout for that, I have had the laser detector go off, but I am usually slow'd by then, I don't speed as much as I used to.<P>You can still get nabbed even with a VG-2 Stealth, I got pulled by a cop with a VG-2 unit, my dector saw it, and cloaked but he was RIGHT behind me and there is no way my unit had enough time to hide.  (takes like 1.5 seconds for it to detect/identify a VG-2 signal then cloak) he pulled me, claimed I had one.  I put it in my coat before I was pulled (it was on the centre console)<P>He asked me, I said "Nope dOnt have one, they are illegal anyway"<P>Then he started asking what the box was on my dash (GPS Receiver), blah blah, what engine is in my car, then drove off.<P><BR>Basically if you can't use one in your juisdiction, don't, or just keep it on your console, I thought about a remote install...||||reg||1
Z||000010||Philphine||11-18-2001||06:20 AM||||never had one, but the trickiest thing i've heard is someone taking apart thier rear view mirror, scraping some of the silver off a corner of the glass and putting the warning lights in it. don't know why i remember it, but i think it was a custom viper install for roseann barr. in one of the car stereo mags.<P>beside the point, but i also heard of the same thing being done with a completly disasembled radio. the display was put behind the mirror glass.||||reg||1
Z||000011||lou||11-18-2001||07:44 AM||||THE ONLY WAY TO GO IS THE VALENTINE ONE WITH THE REMOTE MODULE.  I HAVE MINE MOUNTED UNDER THE OVER HEAD LIGHTS WITH THE MODULE IN THE CENTER OF THE DASH. VALENTINE ONE IS THE ONLY DETECTOR THAT GIVES YOU A LONG ENOUGH WARNING ON LASER AND IS IMPERVIOUS TO LIDAR ( I OFTEN TRAVEL THROUGH DC AND VIRGINIA ).  IT ALSO HAS THE LONGEST WARNING RANGE OF ANY RADAR DETECTOR AND GIVES THE LEAST FALSE ALARMS.  IT TELLS YOU HOW MANY RADARS ARE POINTED AT YOU , FROM WHICH DIRECTION THEY ARE COMING AND WHICH ONE IS THE CLOSEST TO YOU.  THE BEST BAR NONE AND WORTH EVERY PENNY!  THAT IS WHY I HAVE ONE FOR EVERY VEHICLE I OWN.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Cliff Pennock||11-18-2001||08:05 AM||||I will do a custom install soon since radardetectors will be illegal here starting Jan 1st 2002. I've had 6 speeding tickets this year and <B>all</B> were because I was going <B>2</B> (yes, two) miles over the speedlimit.||||reg||1
Z||000013||bHooper||11-18-2001||10:15 AM||||K-40 Remote unit.  These things are the best.  Also a little known fact about the product, you can send them back for "reconditioning" every few years (cost about $50 or so) and the send it back to you with a different serial number on it!... which means, they send you a bran new unit!<P>My friend has their sonaradar one, that tells which direction the bogie is coming in!<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><FONT COLOR="red"><B>Red</B></FONT></FONT> 86 GT 5 speed<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><B>Black</B></FONT> <u>86 SE Automatic</u>||||reg||1
Z||000014||David Bartlett||11-18-2001||10:57 AM||||I thought about heavyer gage wire, unfortunately with 11 wires to control the led readout and 2 for power that would mean one massive cable.<P>The mirror idea sounds great! I wonder If I can find a cheep used mirror at the scrapyard.<P>Keep the idea's coming.||||reg||1
Z||000015||lou||11-18-2001||12:33 PM||||I HAD A K-40, GOT A TICKET WHILE I HAD IT ON, NEVER WARNED ME.  K-40 PAID FOR THE TICKET BUT I SOLD IT AND BOUGHT A VALENTINE ONE.  NEVER HAD A TICKET SINCE, IT HAS A LONGER WARNING RANGE THAN THE K-40 LESS FALSE ALARMS AND A MUCH LONGER RANGE FOR LASER THAN ANYTHING ELSE OUT THER. I SWEAR BY IT AND SO DOES MOTOR TREND AND ROAD AND TRACK.  <A HREF="http://www.valentine1.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.valentine1.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000016||Pyric||11-19-2001||02:07 PM||||I knew a guy that was in the Airforce and got a hold of some of that radar absorbing paint they use on the stealth and painted his ride with it.  Wish I could get a hold of some.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000017||JohnF||11-19-2001||02:25 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Pyric:<BR><B>I knew a guy that was in the Airforce and got a hold of some of that radar absorbing paint they use on the stealth and painted his ride with it.  Wish I could get a hold of some.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>FYI - there is a LOT more to stealth technology than paint! 'Course like our cars, it started with polymer pieces!<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1578096&a=12001112&p=56509969&Sequence=0"> ||||reg||1
Z||000018||NEPTUNE||11-19-2001||02:30 PM||||I have an old passport mounted just above the rearview mirror, wired to the map light. I don't go over 10+ in town, But I do get sporty on the interstate and on country roads. So far so good- 1 tx in last 3 years.  ||||reg||1
Z||000019||Pyric||11-19-2001||05:21 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JohnF:<BR><B> <BR>FYI - there is a LOT more to stealth technology than paint! 'Course like our cars, it started with polymer pieces!<P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Maybe so... but every little bit helps.  Besides, police radar isn't exactly of the same caliber as the military's.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Pyric (edited 11-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000020||DRH||11-19-2001||06:06 PM||||If you just want to make it harder to notice (not totally hidden) what about mounting it between the rear view mirror and light console? Run wires up the A pillar to eliminate the tell-tale power cord. You could even put a switch on the dash to cut power to it without reaching up.<P>It won't be noticable from behind as long as it's off. It won't really be in sight from the side unless they squat down beside the car. <P>Of course this won't help if they are actively looking for it, but could make the difference in a warning and a ticket in places where they aren't illegal, just frowned upon.<P>||||reg||3
Z||000021||David Bartlett||11-19-2001||07:17 PM||||Thats the current location of the bulk of the unit. ||||reg||1
Z||000022||Dslice||11-19-2001||09:29 PM||||In my old 84 coup I have an old(really old) fuzz-buster from the '80s that's the size of a large paper back book. It was too big and heavy to hang from my visor so I put the main unit on top of the glove box(center console) and the read-out where the driver's ash-tray is.<P>------------------<BR>Rick<P>86GT<BR>84COUP<BR>http://home.mn.rr.com/gmfierogt||||reg||1
Z||000023||Red2m4||11-20-2001||03:32 AM||||Is it possible to power a detector off the courtesy lights?  Maybe even use the switches to turn it on and off... ||||reg||1
Z||000024||Nashco||11-20-2001||03:43 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Red2m4:<BR><B>Is it possible to power a detector off the courtesy lights?  Maybe even use the switches to turn it on and off... </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That sounds like a great idea!  Perhaps wire your courtesy lighting system so the two map lights are wired into the right hand side push-button switch, and the left push-button switch powers the radar dector.  You could fiddle with it and easily make it mount up there with the overhead lighting setup.  I think this sounds like the best setup, personally.  I'm not one for radar detectors only because the good ones cost so much, and I've been lucky so far *knock on wood*...<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000025||Flamberge||11-20-2001||04:39 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Pyric:<BR><B>I knew a guy that was in the Airforce and got a hold of some of that radar absorbing paint they use on the stealth and painted his ride with it.  Wish I could get a hold of some.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>This seems highly unlikely. (Not calling you a liar, but questioning your friend.) Even if the guy worked in Paint Shop at a base with the '117 or the B-2, getting a hold of not only the "paint" (which, without getting into it, is actually RADAR Absorbant Material [RAM],) he would also need to use the facility (usually a hanger) to paint his ride. That would mean driving into the hanger, (not too difficult, especially if he already has flightline clearance) and would need to go through the process which I am pretty sure is lengthy. That would mean leaving his car in the hanger overnight, or even worse, for a few days. SPs also regularly check all flightline locales, and if they were to run across a mysterious car in Paint Shop (say, at 3am) they would probably call their flight chief, he calls the SP squadron commander, etc etc. <P>Not that it isn't technically possible, just bloody unlikely. btw...did you ever see this car? What color was it? What was the texture? <P>As for radar detectors, its too bad I can't fit an APG-67 suite in my Fiero's nosecone.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_damon.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR>- Flamberge<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://flamberge.iwarp.com/Serra3sm.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000026||Return of Fiero||11-20-2001||10:24 AM||||You could alway do waht i did for my friend, take the whole damn thing apart, and places the sensor in the front of the detector in the grill, and the rear sensor right in the tail light assembly. then i mounted the guts under the center console, with it hard wired to the cig. lighter (as her doesnt smoke) with the display mounter in a custom plactic box on the dash right above the cluster panel. the box was made so it sloped to the sides, and contured like a curve to the sides of the cluter, and sloped down tothe back, and ended at the dash. i put a "roll up" face plate, simalar to the "hide away" faces to the aftermarket sterios, but it is a manual roll up face. very nice, and he can ride in CT. without a cop saying, "hey,thats a radar detector, i need to ticket you.." its had to get a ticket, if they cant see it... lol<BR>Myke||||reg||1
Z||000027||Pyric||11-20-2001||12:31 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Flamberge:<BR><B> <BR>This seems highly unlikely. (Not calling you a liar, but questioning your friend.)    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_damon.gif"> <P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>He said he was actually on duty (in the Airforce) helping paint one of the planes and they opened too many cans of paint. So they told him to take it so he would not get in trouble for wasteing it.<P>We didn't buy it either until we had a friend of mine who was a state trooper clocked his car to prove him wrong.  The radar gun did not get a response 9 out of 10 times he tried.    <P>I wasn't actually there though, it's just what I was told.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000028||Butter||11-20-2001||12:32 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Pyric:<BR><B>I knew a guy that was in the Airforce and got a hold of some of that radar absorbing paint they use on the stealth and painted his ride with it.  Wish I could get a hold of some.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You wouldn't like the texture or the colour probally and both are important for absorbing the RF. It also does not work for laser units.  The ALQ-117 would be a good jammer to set up for radar but it is too big to fit in a Fiero and requires a power source that is not offered on a normal vehicle.  Besides I wouldn't want to set on top of that much RF when it activates.  This thing tends to smoke radar guns when properly activated.  SP's were warned not to point their gun at a Buff on takeoff! He He He!<P>Flamberge,<BR>I'm sure with the right connections one could get a vehicle painted with the RF absorbing paint.  I saw a beautifully camouflaged brand new Toyota 4x4 truck painted on base.  Thought the guy was crazy to do that to a new truck till he put the clear coat stuff on it and turned it glossy. Looked like a military truck till the gloss application.  Turned out really nice!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000029||Flamberge||11-20-2001||06:27 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Butter:<BR><B> <BR>Flamberge,<BR>I'm sure with the right connections one could get a vehicle painted with the RF absorbing paint.  I saw a beautifully camouflaged brand new Toyota 4x4 truck painted on base.  Thought the guy was crazy to do that to a new truck till he put the clear coat stuff on it and turned it glossy. Looked like a military truck till the gloss application.  Turned out really nice!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Sure, it's possible, but not likely. I would doubt a guy 99.9% of the time who told me that story, unless I saw the car myself. and you're right, the texture is not very aesthetic for a car.<P><P>------------------<BR>- Flamberge<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://flamberge.iwarp.com/Serra3sm.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000030||TylerB11||11-20-2001||07:10 PM||||And as soon as he painted it with the clear coat...bam back to normal. Its all about the surface and the material. I have never ever seen a glossy surface that absorbed radar. I have screwed around with many things, and most have little or no succcess. (My neigbor is a cop, he likes to try out his gun on all my ideas).<P>Radar detectors are by and far the best alternative.<BR>Think about it this way. <BR>Lets take a theoretical radar gun. It has a range of 1,000 feet. To get a reading the signal must go 1,000 feet to the car and 1,000 feet back.  But the radar detector only needs the signal one way. so it can catch that same signal 2,000 ft away. So a radar detector will always pick up a gun a minimum of DOUBLE THE RANGE. it is usually more, because the gun needs a usuable signal, while the detector doesn't.<BR>The way they can get you is instant-on or around a blind turn - as long as you are aware of very radar-friendly areas and slow down, you will be fine.<P>The most important things when choosing a detector:<BR>1. Most states allow them (i think VA and CT are the only ones who  ban then)<BR>2. If you are not in one of those states, VG-2 isn't important (hey you're not breaking the law)<BR>3. It needs to have all bands in current use. (most new guns are K. but it should have X,K,Ka)<BR>4. Laser detector is unneccesary. Radar has a wide beam, while laser's beam is only a couple feet across. Laser also needs a flat surface(read:license plate) and the cop has to be either directly in front of you or behind, unlike radar. Radar measures the doppler shift of the radar waves. Laser measures the change in distance over time - so they are only accurated head on, or tail on, no angles.<BR>5. Even if you get a laser ticket, you can usually fight it. The NJ supreme court has found laser to be innaccurate in most cases (it is).<BR>6. As long as it has the basics, the rest is just icing. Less falses (usually X band) are a plus of more expensive models, some have digital readouts, all sorts of stuff. But the basic Bell detector from radio shack will detect the same as the Valentine. The valetine however will avoid more falses, and give you more information.<P>6. Speeding tickets are easy to get out of. <P>Most local juristictions don't keep up on their radar training and calibration. If you get a ticket, you can subpoena these records. The officer operating the radar must have his training up to date, and the unit must be calibrated every time they go out. Many officers don't even know the physics behind radar. I was reading maxim magazine and they had an article written by a cop and he said "don't even bother with a radar detector because my gun has a range of 1,000 yards (no such one exists, against FCC regs) and i can see you before you see me" as was pointed out above, its obviously false. <P><p>[This message has been edited by TylerB11 (edited 11-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000031||Sootah||11-22-2001||09:30 PM||||Apparently the Valentine is NOT the best one out there... <A HREF="http://www.radartest.com/DS4HEM.html" TARGET=_blank>High-end radar detector shootout</A><P>I'd still like to do a custom install, anybody have pics of theirs?<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sootah__Nuclear_Happy_Face_Sig.jpg">        <BR><sup><A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sootah__Black_Beauty_-_Front_Right.jpg" TARGET=_blank>The Black Beauty</A> (85 GT) - <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sootah__Busted-Ass_SE.jpg" TARGET=_blank>Bustedato</A> (86 SE, Parts car)<BR><I>Never pull your pants down in Cosco, the dang monkeys will getcha</I></sup> <BR><sup><B>AIM</B>: KSSouter<BR><B>MSN IM</B>: I_R_Sootah@hotmail.com<BR></sup>||||reg||1
